  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 107 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern for the long term persists in showing mid to\nupper ridging building over the Intermountain West through next \nweekend. This will result highs holding steady through mid-week in\nthe 90F-95F range higher elevations, 95F-100F along the Pecos \nRiver, northeast Permian Basin, and Presidio Valley, with \n105F-110F readings for the Big Bend. Fortunately, as the upper low\nand its humid core dissipates/develops farther to the west and \nits influence wanes, dew point temperatures are forecast drop \nbelow the 65F-70F range and PWATs to drop below the 1.10\" to 1.20\"\nrange. There will still be a <15% chance of a stray shower/storm \nor two due to less predictable mesoscale boundary formation \ninteracting with heating of elevated terrain, despite the large \nscale sinking and warming/drying motion from the ridge largely \nsuppressing rain chances. 100F-105F readings builds for much of \nthe lowlands apart from the southeast Permian Basin and eastern \nStockton Plateau after mid-week as ridging over the Intermountain \nWest continues to strengthen and expand into the Southern Great \nPlains, while highs rise to 105F-110F for the Presidio Valley and \n110F-115F for the Big Bend. Therefore, anyone outdoors and inside \nvehicles will want to exercise proper heat safety precautions, and\nif possible, avoid spending time outdoors during the hottest part\nof the day, drink plenty of water, and remain in air conditioned \nbuildings to avoid heat stress. Lows rise from the 65F-70F range, \n60F-65F higher elevations Wednesday night back into the 70F-75F \nrange, 65F-70F higher elevations for the overnight periods through\nthe weekend as warmer daytime temperatures take longer to cool \noff.",
